  ],
  "notes": [],
  "dividers": [],
  "blocks": [],
  "meta": {
    "title": "Contains 101 (substring search)",
    "blurb": "Accepts every binary string with 101 somewhere inside. Each state is the longest prefix of the pattern the input currently ends with — the same table the Knuth–Morris–Pratt algorithm builds.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "101",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "0101",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "11011",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "100101",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "100",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "1001",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "110011",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "substring",
        "pattern-matching",
        "kmp"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "A string-search automaton. State *k* means \"the last *k* symbols read are the first *k* symbols of 101\".\n\nThe interesting edges are the ones that go *back*. After **10**, a 0 leaves nothing useful, so the machine returns to the start. But after **1**, another 1 keeps it at \"saw 1\" rather than resetting — the new 1 is itself the start of a possible match. Those back edges are the *failure function* of Knuth–Morris–Pratt, drawn as a diagram.\n\nOnce the pattern has been seen, nothing can undo it: \"found 101\" loops on everything."
    }
  }
}
